      <pose>4.5 3.2 0.6075 0 0 0</pose>
    </model>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         TARGET OBJECTS on the pick workbench

         Three 75 mm spheres in distinct saturated colours; dynamic, so
         Phase 8 can grasp them.

         WHY SPHERES (changed in Phase 7).  These were originally a cube, a
         cylinder and a box, chosen "with distinct colors so YOLO can tell
         them apart".  That premise does not hold: Phase 7 runs the stock
         COCO-pretrained yolov8n.pt, which classifies by learned object
         category, not by colour, and COCO has no class for a coloured
         cube.  Measured on the wrist camera at 0.5-1.0 m, the old
         primitives peaked at 1-3 % confidence — indistinguishable from
         noise — while the tabletop itself was confidently detected as
         "bed"/"dining table".  A plain coloured sphere, by contrast, reads
         as COCO class "sports ball" at 0.7-0.86 confidence from the same
         viewpoint, so the detector does real work instead of being
         hand-fed.  Colour still separates them for a human watching the
         annotated OpenCV window.

         SIZE.  radius 0.033 m = 66 mm across (Phase 7 used 75 mm).  The
         2F-85 opens to 85 mm, so 75 mm left only 5 mm of clearance per pad
         — less than the combined perception, execution and localization
         error, so a descending gripper knocks the ball off the bench
         instead of straddling it.  66 mm doubles that margin to 9.5 mm and
         is still large enough to read as COCO "sports ball".  The matching
         knuckle angle for contact is 0.175 rad; the orchestrator commands
         0.24 rad, i.e. a few mm of interference (gripper_closed).

         HEIGHT.  The Phase 9 workbench top surface is at z = 0.600, so a
         resting ball centre sits at 0.600 + 0.033 = 0.633.  (Phase 7 had
         1.015 + 0.0375 = 1.0525, which is outside the UR5's envelope from
         the closest the base can park — see the workbench comment above.)

         PLACEMENT.  x = 3.92 puts them 0.17 m in from the bench's near
         edge, i.e. 0.68 m in front of a base parked at x = 3.24, which is
         inside the reachable band (0.45-0.85 m) with room for the 0.25 m
         Nav2 goal tolerance either way.

         These are <model> poses, i.e. WORLD coordinates — they are NOT
         relative to pick_workbench.  Each x therefore carries the
         workbench's own x = 4.5; without it the objects spawn in mid-air
         over the robot's home pose at the origin and drop onto its deck.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <model name="target_ball_red">
      <static>false</static>
      <link name="body">
        <inertial>
          <mass>0.2</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>0.0000871</ixx>
            <ixy>0.0</ixy>
            <ixz>0.0</ixz>
            <iyy>0.0000871</iyy>
            <iyz>0.0</iyz>
            <izz>0.0000871</izz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <sphere>
              <radius>0.033</radius>
            </sphere>
          </geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction>
              <ode>
                <mu>1.0</mu>
                <mu2>1.0</mu2>
              </ode>
            </friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <sphere>
              <radius>0.033</radius>
            </sphere>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>1 0 0 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>1 0 0 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
      <pose>3.92 -0.26 0.6330 0 0 0</pose>
    </model>

    <model name="target_ball_blue">
      <static>false</static>
      <link name="body">
        <inertial>
          <mass>0.2</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>0.0000871</ixx>
            <ixy>0.0</ixy>
            <ixz>0.0</ixz>
            <iyy>0.0000871</iyy>
            <iyz>0.0</iyz>
            <izz>0.0000871</izz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <collision name="collision">
